WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.464 --> 00:00:05.410
(birds chirping)
(soft music)

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:09.350
- My relationship to the natural
world was largely fostered

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:14.220
through my grandparents
who owned a farm in Idaho.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.640
We would go there every summer
and we were free to roam

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.543
and play in the forest and hang out.

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:25.160
But I still didn't necessarily think

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.370
I wanted to be a scientist.

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:30.010
It wasn't until I got to understand

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:31.570
the creative part of science,

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.540
that it wasn't just about memorizing,

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.473
like, facts in a text book.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.100
That's what really made me realize,

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.683
oh wait, actually this is a
creative process, and I love it.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:45.630
My name is Kristen Ruegg.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.430
I am an assistant professor
at Colorado State University,

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.110
and I am the co-director of a project

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:54.373
called the Bird Genoscape Project.

00:00:54.373 --> 00:00:56.790
(soft music)

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.880
The goal of the Bird Genoscape Project is

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.780
to track hundreds of the most
critically threatened birds.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.940
All kinds of birds are
part of this project

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:13.270
from tiny songbirds, like
the willow flycatcher,

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.093
to my favorite, the
charismatic burrowing owl.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:23.050
I think it's amazing that
over half of the birds

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:27.150
that are in North America actually leave

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.300
during the winter months
and go somewhere else

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:34.300
and spend the majority of
the year actually not here.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.120
Birds migrate south in the winter

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.490
in search of better weather and more food,

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:50.500
and they migrate back north in the spring

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:54.003
in search of better habitat for breeding.

00:01:55.370 --> 00:02:00.263
The main mystery with birds
is where do birds migrate?

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.800
The ability to track
migratory birds has been

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.820
a huge challenge for centuries.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:12.030
One of the challenges is
there are tracking devices,

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.500
but they're usually not small enough

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.113
to be able to put on a migratory bird.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:24.410
So our thought was, well, if
we could develop a technology

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:29.030
that could use information
within a single feather,

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.270
like information in the DNA of the bird

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:32.810
to track their migratory movements,

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.300
then we could get
information from every bird

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.413
that comes through a monitoring station.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.380
If I wanted to understand
where my ancestors were from,

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:45.630
I might take a DNA sample from me

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.410
and send it off to a
genetic ancestry service.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:52.620
They would compare my DNA
against this huge database

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.270
of DNA from many, many other people

00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:01.177
and be able to identify
that my most likely ancestry

00:03:01.177 --> 00:03:03.660
is largely Norwegian.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.790
In a sense, that's what we're doing

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:10.230
with the DNA found in
a single bird feather.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:14.093
It's like genetic ancestry
mapping for birds.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.430
So here we have an American robin,

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:20.720
one of our Genoscape species.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.490
This robin, yes, I see you.

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:28.000
This robin migrates from the boreal forest

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.240
in Canada and parts of the US

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.633
and goes as far south
in the winter as Mexico.

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:37.250
And we also collect the
feathers, the tail feathers,

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.876
that we use for genetic analysis.

00:03:39.876 --> 00:03:42.709
(uplifting music)

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.070
The reason why we collect these feathers

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:58.180
is because we can use the tip
of the feather right here,

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:00.130
which contains a little bit of DNA

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:04.010
to tell you which migratory
pathway this bird took

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:06.563
and where it winters and breeds.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.940
So what you have displayed here

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.660
is the map for the Wilson's
Warbler showing the location

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.610
of the six populations.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:19.940
Each one goes to a
different wintering area.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:22.300
For example, the Rocky
Mountain population heads

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.743
all the way down to El
Salvador and Panama.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.830
Using their DNA, we can follow
their migratory journey.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:31.603
All we need is DNA from
the tip of a feather.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.890
Now that the Bird Genoscape
Project is helping us understand

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:39.060
where and how birds move
throughout the year,

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.370
we can figure out the best ways

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:42.900
to help them survive in a changing world.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.060
Uncovering the mysteries of bird migration

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.145
may be the key to protecting them.

00:04:47.145 --> 00:04:49.978
(uplifting music)

